A||||1||gtsonly||Valve burning oil- need help!!!!!||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||05-22-2001||07:34 PM||||I have a 86Gt with 82,000 miles on it. 2.8 stock. It has been burning oil for sometime now. Up til this weekend I thought it was rings but after a compression check, 1 through 5 at 110 to 115 and #6 at 95. I called some mech's and they say it is leaking through the valves so I am looking to do a valve job on the GT. How much power loss could come of a condition like this? The car has power but just does not seem to be all there. Any help?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||05-23-2001||12:51 AM||||If your main concern is the oil consumption, try replacing the valve seals first; it might cure most of your troubles without having to pull the heads off.  While you have each valve spring off, check the fit of its valve in the valve guide - if it's obviously loose in the guide, you'll probably have to pull the heads anyway.<P>I think those compression readings sound low, but not terrible for an old motor; be sure to recheck compression with throttle propped open - the readings might increase.  A leakdown test can confirm whether or not the valves are leaking.<P>I hate to tear down a motor if I don't have to, because it's so time-consuming to rebuild a motor, and I often wonder if something simpler might do the trick.  Do a thorough tuneup, before you decide you need a rebuild.  It would be frustrating to go through the whole process of a rebuild, then find that you needed an ignition module to bring back the performance you expect.<P>On the other hand, if you're dead-set on doing a rebuild, go ahead.  Just count on a fair amount of work and downtime.     ||||reg||1
